IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wa-interpretation-time`

-- Interpretation Act 1984 (Western Australia) -- PART X: MISCELLANEOUS PROVISIONS (ss 73 to 77), and the two other notes
-- that carry operative text: note 1 (section 11 of the Interpretation Act 1918, kept alive by s 77(2)) and note 4
-- (section 47 of that Act and its Second Schedule, kept alive by s 77(4)).
-- Section 75(1) (the day service by post is deemed effected) is in wa-interpretation-time.l4, and so are sections B to E
-- of the Second Schedule (how long after an offence a complaint may be made).

§ `Part X -- Miscellaneous provisions`

§§ `73. State deemed established 1 June 1829`

-- Section 73: for determining whether any Imperial Act applies within the State, the State is deemed to have been
-- established on 1 June 1829.  The text does not say what follows from the date (which Acts apply); it fixes the date.
-- src:700 | For the purpose of determining the applicability or otherwise within the State of any Imperial Act, the State shall be deemed to have been established on 1 June 1829.
@ref s 73 (src:700)
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the State is deemed to have been established, for the applicability of Imperial Acts` MEANS Date 1 6 1829

-- The comparison a person would make with it: an Imperial Act enacted before that day.  What follows is not in the text.
@ref s 73 (src:700)
GIVEN `the day the Imperial Act was enacted` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the Imperial Act was enacted before the State is deemed to have been established` `the day the Imperial Act was enacted` MEANS
    Day `the day the Imperial Act was enacted` LESS THAN Day `the day the State is deemed to have been established, for the applicability of Imperial Acts`

§§ `74. Prescribed forms, certain deviations do not invalidate`

-- Section 74: where a form is prescribed or specified under a written law, deviations from it not materially affecting
-- the substance nor likely to mislead do not invalidate the form used.  A deviation that does either is not dealt with.
-- src:702 | Where a form is prescribed or specified under a written law, deviations therefrom not materially affecting the substance nor likely to mislead shall not invalidate the form used.
@ref s 74 (src:702)
GIVEN `the deviation materially affects the substance` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the deviation is likely to mislead` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the deviation invalidates the form used` `the deviation materially affects the substance` `the deviation is likely to mislead` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the deviation materially affects the substance`) AND (NOT `the deviation is likely to mislead`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `75. Service of documents by post (subsections (2) and (3))`

-- Section 75(2): where a written law authorises or requires a document to be served by registered post, then, if the
-- document is eligible and acceptable for transmission as certified mail, service may be effected either by registered
-- post or by certified mail.  Section 75(3): this applies unless the contrary intention appears, and does not apply where
-- the written law requires production of an acknowledgment signed by the person to whom the document was addressed to the
-- effect that it was delivered to that person.
-- src:705 | (2) Where a written law authorises or requires a document to be served by registered post, whether the word “serve” or any of the words “give”, “deliver”, or “send” or any other similar word or expression is used, then, if the document is eligible and acceptable for transmission as certified mail, the service of the document may be effected either by registered post or by certified mail.
-- src:706 | (3) Subsections (1) and (2) apply unless the contrary intention appears and subsection (2) does not apply where a written law requires the production of an acknowledgment signed by a person to whom a document was addressed to the effect that the document was delivered to that person.
@ref s 75(2) (src:705)
@ref s 75(3) (src:706)
GIVEN `a written law authorises or requires the document to be served by registered post` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the document is eligible and acceptable for transmission as certified mail` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the written law requires production of an acknowledgment signed by the person addressed that the document was delivered to that person` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`service may be effected either by registered post or by certified mail` `a written law authorises or requires the document to be served by registered post` `the document is eligible and acceptable for transmission as certified mail` `a contrary intention appears` `the written law requires production of an acknowledgment signed by the person addressed that the document was delivered to that person` MEANS
    IF     `a written law authorises or requires the document to be served by registered post`
       AND `the document is eligible and acceptable for transmission as certified mail`
       AND (NOT `a contrary intention appears`)
       AND (NOT `the written law requires production of an acknowledgment signed by the person addressed that the document was delivered to that person`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Section 75(3), first limb, for subsection (1): it applies unless the contrary intention appears.
@ref s 75(3) (src:706)
GIVEN `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 75(1) applies` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    NOT (`a contrary intention appears`)

§§ `76. Service of documents generally`

DECLARE `Manner of serving a document` IS ONE OF
    `delivering the document to the person personally`
    `post in accordance with section 75(1)`
    `leaving it at the person's usual or last known place of abode`
    `leaving it at the usual or last known place of business of a person who is a principal of a business`
    `delivering, leaving or posting it, addressed to a corporation or association, at its principal place of business or principal office in the State`
    `some other manner`

-- Section 76: where a written law authorises or requires a document to be served without directing a particular manner,
-- service may be effected in the manners (a) to (d).  Manner (c)'s business limb is open to a principal of a business, and
-- manner (d) to a corporation or an association of persons (incorporated or not).  Manners (a) to (c) are open to every person,
-- a corporation included (fork F10 in NOTES.md).  Another manner is not dealt with.
-- src:708 | Where a written law authorises or requires a document to be served, whether the word “serve” or any of the words “give”, “deliver”, or “send” or any other similar word or expression is used, without directing it to be served in a particular manner, service of that document may be effected on the person to be served —
-- src:709 | (a) by delivering the document to him personally; or
-- src:710 | (b) by post in accordance with section 75(1); or
-- src:711 | (c) by leaving it for him at his usual or last known place of abode, or if he is a principal of a business, at his usual or last known place of business; or
-- src:712 | (d) in the case of a corporation or of an association of persons (whether incorporated or not), by delivering or leaving the document or posting it as a letter, addressed in each case to the corporation or association, at its principal place of business or principal office in the State.
@ref s 76 (src:708-712)
GIVEN manner IS A `Manner of serving a document`
      `the written law directs the document to be served in a particular manner` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person to be served is a principal of a business` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person to be served is a corporation or an association of persons, incorporated or not` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`service may be effected in the manner` manner `the written law directs the document to be served in a particular manner` `the person to be served is a principal of a business` `the person to be served is a corporation or an association of persons, incorporated or not` MEANS
    IF `the written law directs the document to be served in a particular manner`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE
        CONSIDER manner
        WHEN `delivering the document to the person personally` THEN JUST TRUE
        WHEN `post in accordance with section 75(1)` THEN JUST TRUE
        WHEN `leaving it at the person's usual or last known place of abode` THEN JUST TRUE
        WHEN `leaving it at the usual or last known place of business of a person who is a principal of a business` THEN JUST `the person to be served is a principal of a business`
        WHEN `delivering, leaving or posting it, addressed to a corporation or association, at its principal place of business or principal office in the State` THEN JUST `the person to be served is a corporation or an association of persons, incorporated or not`
        WHEN `some other manner` THEN NOTHING

§§ `76A. Written laws made before Australia Acts, validity of`

-- Section 76A(1): each provision of an Act or subsidiary legislation enacted or made, or purporting to have been, before
-- the commencement of the Australia Acts has the same effect, and is as valid, as it would have been if the Australia Acts
-- had been in operation then.  The Australia Acts are the Australia Act 1986 of the Commonwealth and of the United Kingdom
-- (s 5); the text does not give their commencement day, so the caller says whether the provision came before it.
-- src:714 | (1) Each provision of an Act or subsidiary legislation enacted or made, or purporting to have been enacted or made, before the commencement of the Australia Acts —
-- src:715 | (a) has the same effect as it would have had; and
-- src:716 | (b) is as valid as it would have been,
-- src:717 | if the Australia Acts had been in operation at the time of its enactment or making, or purported enactment or making.
@ref s 76A(1) (src:714-717)
GIVEN `the provision was enacted or made, or purported to be, before the commencement of the Australia Acts` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the provision has the effect and validity it would have had if the Australia Acts had been in operation then` `the provision was enacted or made, or purported to be, before the commencement of the Australia Acts` MEANS
    IF `the provision was enacted or made, or purported to be, before the commencement of the Australia Acts` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Result section 76A(1) is asked to bring about` IS ONE OF
    `invalidating an enactment that was valid immediately before the commencement of the Australia Acts`
    `invalidating an Act because it was assented to by the Sovereign rather than the Governor`
    `some other result`

-- Section 76A(2): subsection (1) is not intended to, and is not to be given effect so as to, bring about either of the two
-- results in (a) and (b).  Any other result is not dealt with.
-- src:718 | (2) Subsection (1) is not intended to, and is not to be given effect so as to —
-- src:719 | (a) invalidate any enactment that was valid immediately before the commencement of the Australia Acts; or
-- src:720 | (b) invalidate any Act because it was assented to by the Sovereign rather than the Governor.
@ref s 76A(2) (src:718-720)
GIVEN x IS A `Result section 76A(1) is asked to bring about`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`subsection (1) is to be given effect so as to bring it about` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `some other result` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§§ `77. Repeal and saving`

-- Section 77(2): section 11 of the Interpretation Act 1918 continues to apply to Acts passed before the commencement of
-- this Act (note 1 says: before 1 July 1984).  An Act passed on 1 July 1984 is not before the commencement, which is the
-- beginning of that day (s 21).
-- src:724 | (2) Notwithstanding subsection (1), section 11 of the Interpretation Act 1918 shall continue to apply to Acts passed before the commencement of this Act 1.
@ref s 77(2) (src:724)
GIVEN `the day the Act was passed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 11 of the Interpretation Act 1918 continues to apply to the Act` `the day the Act was passed` MEANS
    Day `the day the Act was passed` LESS THAN Day `the day this Act came into operation`

-- Section 77(3): the enactment of a provision of this Act expressed to apply to Acts or subsidiary legislation passed or
-- made after the commencement is not presumed to have amended the law so as to affect the construction of an Act or
-- subsidiary legislation passed or made before that enactment.
-- src:725 | (3) The enactment of a provision of this Act that is expressed to apply to Acts or subsidiary legislation passed or made after the commencement of this Act shall not be presumed to have amended the law so as to affect the construction of any Act or subsidiary legislation passed or made before such enactment.
@ref s 77(3) (src:725)
GIVEN `the provision is expressed to apply to Acts or subsidiary legislation passed or made after the commencement of this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the earlier Act or subsidiary legislation was passed or made before the enactment of the provision` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the enactment is presumed to have amended the law so as to affect the construction of the earlier law` `the provision is expressed to apply to Acts or subsidiary legislation passed or made after the commencement of this Act` `the earlier Act or subsidiary legislation was passed or made before the enactment of the provision` MEANS
    IF     `the provision is expressed to apply to Acts or subsidiary legislation passed or made after the commencement of this Act`
       AND `the earlier Act or subsidiary legislation was passed or made before the enactment of the provision`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Section 77(4): section 47(2) of the Interpretation Act 1918 and the Second Schedule to that Act continue to apply to any
-- Act to which section 47 applied immediately before the commencement of this Act (note 4).
-- src:726 | (4) Notwithstanding subsection (1), section 47(2) of the Interpretation Act 1918 and the Second Schedule to that Act shall continue to apply to any Act to which that section applied immediately before the commencement of this Act 4.
@ref s 77(4) (src:726)
GIVEN `section 47 of the Interpretation Act 1918 applied to the Act immediately before the commencement of this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 47(2) and the Second Schedule of the Interpretation Act 1918 continue to apply to the Act` `section 47 of the Interpretation Act 1918 applied to the Act immediately before the commencement of this Act` MEANS
    `section 47 of the Interpretation Act 1918 applied to the Act immediately before the commencement of this Act`

§§ `Note 1: section 11 of the Interpretation Act 1918`

-- Section 11(1): where an Act is not to come into operation immediately on its passing, and confers a power (or will, on
-- coming into operation, amend another Act to confer one) to make an appointment, hold an election, make or issue an
-- instrument, give notices, prescribe forms or do any other thing for the purposes of the Act, the power may, unless the
-- Act otherwise provides, be exercised at any time after the passing.
-- src:937 | (1) Where an Act is not to come into operation immediately on the passing thereof, and confers power or will, upon its coming into operation, amend another Act so as to confer power under the other Act as so amended, to make any appointment, to hold any election, to make, grant or issue any instrument that is to say any proclamation, Order in Council, order, warrant, scheme, letters patent, rules, regulations, or bylaws, to give notices, to prescribe forms, or to do any other thing for the purposes of the Act, the power that is or will be conferred may, unless the Act otherwise provides, be exercised at any time after the passing of the firstmentioned Act but the exercise of the power does not confer a right or impose an obligation upon a person before the coming into operation of the firstmentioned Act except in so far as is necessary or expedient for the purpose of making the firstmentioned Act fully effective upon its coming into operation or the other Act fully effective when so amended.
@ref note 1 s 11(1) (src:937)
GIVEN `the Act is not to come into operation immediately on its passing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Act confers the power, or will on coming into operation amend another Act to confer it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Act otherwise provides` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the power may be exercised at any time after the passing of the Act` `the Act is not to come into operation immediately on its passing` `the Act confers the power, or will on coming into operation amend another Act to confer it` `the Act otherwise provides` MEANS
    IF     `the Act is not to come into operation immediately on its passing`
       AND `the Act confers the power, or will on coming into operation amend another Act to confer it`
       AND (NOT `the Act otherwise provides`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Section 11(1), the limit: the exercise does not confer a right or impose an obligation on a person before the Act comes
-- into operation, except in so far as is necessary or expedient to make the Act (or the amended Act) fully effective then.
@ref note 1 s 11(1) (src:937)
GIVEN `it is necessary or expedient for making the Act or the amended Act fully effective upon its coming into operation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the exercise may confer a right or impose an obligation before the Act comes into operation` `it is necessary or expedient for making the Act or the amended Act fully effective upon its coming into operation` MEANS
    `it is necessary or expedient for making the Act or the amended Act fully effective upon its coming into operation`

-- Section 11(2): a power purportedly exercised before the day the Interpretation Act Amendment Act 1975 came into operation,
-- for an Act that came into operation before that day, is deemed exercised as validly as if the section as re-enacted by
-- that Amendment Act had been in operation then.  The text does not give that day; the caller says whether it was before.
-- src:938 | (2) A power purporting to have been exercised at a time before the date of the coming into operation of the Interpretation Act Amendment Act 1975 for the purposes of an Act that came into operation before that date, shall be deemed to have been exercised as validly and effectually as it would have been if this section as reenacted by the Interpretation Act Amendment Act 1975 had been in operation at that time.
@ref note 1 s 11(2) (src:938)
GIVEN `the power purported to be exercised before the day the Interpretation Act Amendment Act 1975 came into operation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Act it was exercised for came into operation before that day` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the exercise is deemed as valid and effectual as if section 11 as re-enacted in 1975 had been in operation` `the power purported to be exercised before the day the Interpretation Act Amendment Act 1975 came into operation` `the Act it was exercised for came into operation before that day` MEANS
    IF `the power purported to be exercised before the day the Interpretation Act Amendment Act 1975 came into operation` AND `the Act it was exercised for came into operation before that day`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Section 11(3): in section 11 a reference to an Act includes a reference to a provision or provisions of an Act.
-- src:939 | (3) In this section a reference to an Act includes a reference to a provision or provisions of an Act.
@ref note 1 s 11(3) (src:939)
GIVEN `it is a provision or provisions of an Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a reference to an Act in section 11 includes it` `it is a provision or provisions of an Act` MEANS
    `it is a provision or provisions of an Act`

§§ `Note 4: section 47 of the Interpretation Act 1918 and its Second Schedule`

-- Section 47(1): any of the provisions of the sections of the Second Schedule, distinguished by the letters A to H, may be
-- incorporated as enactments in any Act by reference to the Schedule and the letters.
-- src:946 | (1) Any of the provisions contained in the sections of the Second Schedule hereto, and distinguished respectively by the letters A, B, C, D, E, F, G, and H, may be incorporated as enactments in any Act by reference to the said Schedule and to the letters distinguishing the said sections respectively.
@ref note 4 s 47(1) (src:946)
GIVEN `the Act refers to the Second Schedule and to the letter distinguishing the section` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the section of the Second Schedule is incorporated as an enactment in the Act` `the Act refers to the Second Schedule and to the letter distinguishing the section` MEANS
    `the Act refers to the Second Schedule and to the letter distinguishing the section`

DECLARE `Schedule an Act passed before 1 July 1984 refers to` IS ONE OF
    `the Second Schedule to the Interpretation Act 1898`
    `the Schedule to the Shortening Ordinance 1853`
    `some other schedule`

-- Section 47(2): where an Act passed before the commencement of the 1918 Act refers to the sections, distinguished by
-- letters, of the Second Schedule to the Interpretation Act 1898 or of the Schedule to the Shortening Ordinance 1853, the
-- reference is deemed to be to the corresponding sections of the Second Schedule to the 1918 Act.
-- src:947 | (2) Where in any Act passed before the commencement of this Act reference is made to the sections (distinguished by letters as aforesaid) of the Second Schedule to the Interpretation Act 1898 or of the Schedule to the Shortening Ordinance 1853, such reference shall be deemed to be made to the corresponding sections of the Second Schedule to this Act.
@ref note 4 s 47(2) (src:947)
GIVEN x IS A `Schedule an Act passed before 1 July 1984 refers to`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the reference is deemed to be to the corresponding section of the Second Schedule to the Interpretation Act 1918` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `some other schedule` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- Second Schedule, A: informations, complaints and proceedings for offences made summarily punishable on conviction before
-- Justices of the Peace in Petty Sessions are heard and determined, and penalties and forfeitures enforced and appropriated,
-- according to the Justices Act 1902.
-- src:950 | All informations, complaints, and proceedings in respect of offences against this Act hereby made summarily punishable upon conviction before a Justice or Justices of the Peace in Petty Sessions shall be heard and determined, and the penalties and forfeitures in respect of the same be enforced and appropriated (if not hereinbefore otherwise appropriated) according to the provisions of the Justices Act 1902.
@ref note 4 Second Schedule A (src:950)
GIVETH A STRING
`the Act by which section A of the Second Schedule says informations, complaints and proceedings are heard and penalties enforced` MEANS "Justices Act 1902"

DECLARE `Ground an order, judgment, warrant or other proceeding is attacked on` IS ONE OF
    `want of form only`
    `removal by certiorari, or by any writ or process whatsoever, into any superior Court of the State`
    `some other ground`

-- Second Schedule, F: no order, judgment, warrant or other proceeding made, or purporting to be made, under or concerning
-- the conviction of any offender against the Act is to be quashed or vacated for want of form only, or removed or
-- removable by certiorari or any writ or process into a superior Court of the State.
-- src:960 | No order, judgment, warrant, or other proceeding made, or purporting to be made under or concerning the conviction of any offender against this Act shall be quashed or vacated for want of form only, or be removed or removable by certiorari, or by any writ or process whatsoever into any superior Court of the State.
@ref note 4 Second Schedule F (src:960)
GIVEN x IS A `Ground an order, judgment, warrant or other proceeding is attacked on`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the proceeding may be quashed, vacated or removed on that ground` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `some other ground` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- Second Schedule, G, first limb: in an action against a person acting in execution of the Act for anything done under it,
-- no plaintiff recovers if tender of sufficient amends was made before the action was brought, or a sufficient sum was
-- paid into Court after it was brought, with costs incurred to that time.
-- src:962 | And, for the protection of persons acting in execution of this Act, where an action is commenced against any one of such persons for anything done under this Act, in every such action the defendant may plead the general issue and give this Act and the special matter in evidence at any trial to be had hereupon, and no plaintiff shall recover in any such action if tender of sufficient amends shall have been made before such action brought, or if a sufficient sum of money shall have been paid into Court, after such action brought, by, or on behalf of the defendant, together with costs incurred up to that time; and if a verdict shall pass for the defendant or the plaintiff become nonsuited or discontinue such action after issue joined, or if, upon demurrer or otherwise, judgment shall be given against the plaintiff, the defendant shall recover his full costs as between solicitor and client, and have the like remedy for the same as any defendant hath by law in other cases; and, though a verdict shall be given for the plaintiff in any such action, such plaintiff shall not have costs against the defendant unless the Judge before whom the trial is held shall certify his approbation of the action and of the verdict obtained thereon.
@ref note 4 Second Schedule G (src:962)
GIVEN `tender of sufficient amends was made before the action was brought` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a sufficient sum, with costs incurred up to that time, was paid into Court after the action was brought` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the plaintiff recovers in the action` `tender of sufficient amends was made before the action was brought` `a sufficient sum, with costs incurred up to that time, was paid into Court after the action was brought` MEANS
    IF `tender of sufficient amends was made before the action was brought` OR `a sufficient sum, with costs incurred up to that time, was paid into Court after the action was brought`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `How an action against a person acting in execution of the Act ended` IS ONE OF
    `a verdict passed for the defendant`
    `the plaintiff became nonsuited or discontinued after issue joined`
    `judgment was given against the plaintiff upon demurrer or otherwise`
    `a verdict was given for the plaintiff`

-- Second Schedule, G, second limb: if a verdict passes for the defendant, or the plaintiff is nonsuited or discontinues
-- after issue joined, or judgment is given against the plaintiff on demurrer or otherwise, the defendant recovers full
-- costs as between solicitor and client.  Where a verdict is given for the plaintiff, the plaintiff has no costs against
-- the defendant unless the Judge certifies approbation of the action and the verdict; with the certificate the paragraph
-- does not bar costs, and says no more.
@ref note 4 Second Schedule G (src:962)
GIVEN x IS A `How an action against a person acting in execution of the Act ended`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the defendant recovers full costs as between solicitor and client` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `a verdict was given for the plaintiff` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@ref note 4 Second Schedule G (src:962)
GIVEN `the Judge before whom the trial is held certifies approbation of the action and of the verdict obtained` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the plaintiff who obtained a verdict has costs against the defendant` `the Judge before whom the trial is held certifies approbation of the action and of the verdict obtained` MEANS
    IF `the Judge before whom the trial is held certifies approbation of the action and of the verdict obtained` THEN NOTHING ELSE JUST FALSE

-- Second Schedule, H, first limb: no action lies against a Justice of the Peace, Officer of Police, Policeman, Constable,
-- Peace Officer or other person in the Government's employ authorised to carry the provisions of the Act into effect, or any
-- person acting for or under such a person, on account of anything done or to be done or commanded in carrying the
-- provisions of the Act into effect against parties offending or suspected of offending, unless there is direct proof of
-- corruption or malice.
-- src:964 | No action shall lie against any Justice of the Peace, Officer of Police, Policeman, Constable, Peace Officer, or any other person in the employ of the Government authorised to carry the provisions of this Act, or any of them, into effect, or any person acting for, or under such persons, or any of them, on account of any act, matter, or thing done, or to be done, or commanded by them, or any of them, in carrying the provisions of this Act into effect again any parties offending or suspected of offending against the same, unless there is direct proof of corruption or malice; and if any such person shall be sued for any act, matter, or thing which he shall have so done, or shall so do, in carrying the provisions of this Act into effect, he may plead the general issue and give the special matter in evidence; and in case of judgment after verdict, or by a Judge sitting as a jury, or on demurrer being given for the defendant, or of the plaintiff discontinuing, or becoming nonsuit in any such action, the Court before which the action was brought may award treble costs to the defendant or such portion of those costs as the Court thinks fit.
@ref note 4 Second Schedule H (src:964)
GIVEN `the defendant is one of the persons the paragraph names, or a person acting for or under one of them` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the thing was done, or to be done, or commanded in carrying the provisions of the Act into effect against parties offending or suspected of offending` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there is direct proof of corruption or malice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`an action lies against the defendant` `the defendant is one of the persons the paragraph names, or a person acting for or under one of them` `the thing was done, or to be done, or commanded in carrying the provisions of the Act into effect against parties offending or suspected of offending` `there is direct proof of corruption or malice` MEANS
    IF     `the defendant is one of the persons the paragraph names, or a person acting for or under one of them`
       AND `the thing was done, or to be done, or commanded in carrying the provisions of the Act into effect against parties offending or suspected of offending`
       AND (NOT `there is direct proof of corruption or malice`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `How an action against a person the paragraph protects ended` IS ONE OF
    `judgment after verdict for the defendant`
    `judgment by a Judge sitting as a jury for the defendant`
    `judgment on demurrer for the defendant`
    `the plaintiff discontinued`
    `the plaintiff became nonsuit`
    `judgment for the plaintiff`

-- Second Schedule, H, second limb: where such a person is sued and judgment goes for the defendant (after verdict, by a
-- Judge sitting as a jury, or on demurrer), or the plaintiff discontinues or is nonsuit, the Court may award the defendant
-- treble costs or such portion of them as it thinks fit.  Where judgment is for the plaintiff the paragraph says nothing.
@ref note 4 Second Schedule H (src:964)
GIVEN x IS A `How an action against a person the paragraph protects ended`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the Court may award the defendant treble costs or such portion of them as it thinks fit` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `judgment for the plaintiff` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
